This issue stays OPEN as the tracker for those two residual items.
Next: the cadaptor forks; the empty.dwg regen is a one-shot runner task.

* MEASURED 2026-08-14 — empty.dwg is NOT the blocker

The CAD-tagged jobs ran for the first time on 2026-08-14 (they had been
queued against =bricscad= / =autocad= tags that no runner carries; one
=cad= tag on the concurrency=1 runner fixed that). What they show
contradicts the "empty.dwg regen" line above, so it is corrected here
rather than acted on.

The file exists, is wired, and WORKS. =PROBE_DWG= is
=c:/gitlab-runner/dwg/empty.dwg= in =.gitlab/native.yml=, and in the same
pipeline (2761360851) three jobs consumed it successfully:

| encoding:experiment:autocad:windows  | SUCCESS |
| pathname:probe:autocad:windows       | SUCCESS |
| pathname:probe:bricscad:windows      | SUCCESS |

And the two vendor-probe jobs that FAILED opened it without complaint:

: autocad  — protocol: matched "READY" after 0.78 s
: bricscad — protocol: matched "READY" after 9.40 s

Both then ran their server loop to a normal return and produced:

:   observations: 0 conforms, 0 known-divergence, 0 unexpected, N missing
:   (no OBSERVE lines — backend unreachable or probe crashed)

So the drawing opens, the runtime bootstraps, the loop runs — and NO
OBSERVE line is emitted. On BOTH vendors. Regenerating empty.dwg cannot
change that.

** What it is not

Not the =&rest= gap either, tempting as it looks. The AutoCAD trace does
log =[CAD] host lacks &rest; keeping bootstrap 1-arg shadows +
walk-rewriting normalize= — and the vendor harvest in the same job
confirms AutoCAD rejects it (=EXTPROBE REST-BINDS=ERROR:nombre
d'arguments trop important=). But BricsCAD HAS =&rest=, takes no such
fallback, and loses its OBSERVE lines identically. A cause common to both
is upstream of that.

** FOUND 2026-08-14 — the log was UTF-16 and the comparator read UTF-8

The observation channel it was. Diagnosed entirely from the archived
artifacts of the failed run, without re-running anything.

The chain is: probe =princ='s =OBSERVE <name> <token>=, alfe bridges the
CAD's output into =protocol/stdout.txt= and relays it to its own stdout,
the PowerShell harness tees that into =<scenario>.log=, and
=scripts/compare-observations.py= parses the log. Every link worked. The
last one silently did not:

: $ python3 -c "b=open('entity-lifecycle.log','rb').read(); ..."
: BOM b'\xff\xfe'
: ascii  OBSERVE occurrences: 0
: utf16  OBSERVE occurrences: 101
: NUL bytes: 7596

Windows PowerShell's =Tee-Object -FilePath= writes UTF-16LE with a BOM.
The comparator opened the log with =encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"=,
which does NOT raise — it yields a wall of replacement characters. Every
OBSERVE line vanished, the table came out empty, and the script printed
"(no OBSERVE lines — backend unreachable or probe crashed)", accusing a
CAD that had just emitted 101 perfectly good observations.

A warning for whoever reads this next: =grep= misled the first pass.
=ugrep=, the grep in use here, transparently decodes UTF-16, so grepping
the log found all 101 OBSERVE lines and made it look like the harness was
being handed good data. Only =od= / a byte count settled it. When a text
tool disagrees with a program about a file, check the bytes.

** The result: every vendor probe PASSES, on both engines

Re-running the comparator over the SAME archived logs, with the decode
fixed (1.8.36):

| probe            | AutoCAD                  | BricsCAD                              |
|------------------+--------------------------+---------------------------------------|
| entity-lifecycle | 101 conforms, 0 missing  | 97 conforms + 4 known-divergence      |
| drawing-data     | 27 conforms, 0 missing   | 26 conforms + 1 known-divergence      |
| selection        | 21 conforms, 0 missing   | 21 conforms, 0 missing                |

Exit 0 on all six. And the divergences are not noise — they are exactly
the two this register documents, now confirmed against real hardware
rather than inferred:

- D1: =d1.markerless-{ellipse,lwpolyline,ray,xline}: created (normative
  rejected)= — BricsCAD accepts ENTMAKE data with the R13+ subclass
  markers omitted, where the spec (per AutoCAD) requires them. Precisely
  the =[entmake-subclass-marker]= entry.
- D3: =dict.entmod-xrecord-entry: payload2 (normative payload)= —
  BricsCAD applies ENTMOD to a non-graphical object where AutoCAD makes
  it a no-op. Precisely the =[entmod-nongraphical]= entry.

The AutoCAD side also harvested, in the same run, direct confirmation of
what the portability register had only inferred: =EXTPROBE
SETF-STRING=ERROR:no function definition: SETF=, =LET-SUM=ERROR:no
function definition: Y=, =REST-BINDS=ERROR:nombre d'arguments trop
important=. AutoCAD has no SETF, no LET, and rejects &REST.

So the "selection re-verify" this ticket lists as residual is DONE —
21/21 on both vendors — and it never needed a new empty.dwg.

** On sourcing a blank drawing, when one IS needed

Recorded because the question was asked (pjb) and the answer is not
obvious:

- Do NOT commit a .dwg. It is a vendor binary bound to a format version,
  and this project already decided against shipping one (see
  libredwg-empty-dwg-write-invalid: "the vendor probes use the runner's
  CAD-made empty.dwg + clean-slate, no generated DWG shipped").
- Do NOT generate it with libredwg: its writer emits files real CADs
  reject (AcDbDictionary where AcDbLayerTable is expected; AutoCAD
  ErrorStatus=53). That is the whole subject of that ticket.
- PREFER the templates the products ship, which is what pjb suggested and
  what the code already does: =discover-bricscad-template= and
  =discover-autocad-template= (the latter looking for
  =Template/acadiso.dwt=) exist in the two backends today.
- A provisioning job that opens a shipped template under accoreconsole
  and SAVEAS to .dwg is the right fallback IF a real =.dwg= is required
  rather than a =.dwt=. It is a one-shot runner task, not per-pipeline
  work.
